<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!-- The app talks to the game server over the network. Unity usually adds this itself once it sees
         networking code, but declaring it here means it can't go missing. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!-- android:usesCleartextTraffic="true" is what your question 4.2 was about: it goes as an ATTRIBUTE
         on <application>, not as its own element. Since Android 9 (API 28) plain HTTP is blocked by
         default, and our dev server is http://<pc-ip>:5238 — without this the phone fails to connect
         with a bare "cleartext not permitted" and nothing else to go on.
         DEV ONLY: put the server behind HTTPS for a real release and delete this attribute. -->
    <!-- TREAT THIS AS A GAME (`BL-46`, owner 2026-08-14: *"need only to be able to make it as a game -
         threat it as a game - to enter the game launcher on its own and to be able to use the game boost
         fetures"*). Two attributes, because the OEM launchers do not all read the same one:

           android:appCategory="game" — the modern signal (API 26+). Samsung's Game Launcher / Game
             Booster, Android's own game-mode / "Game Dashboard" and the per-app performance profiles
             all classify on this. It is what unlocks the in-game touch-block overlay, do-not-disturb
             while playing, the FPS/temperature panel and the performance-vs-battery slider.
           android:isGame="true" — the older flag (API 21, deprecated by appCategory in 26 but still a
             valid attribute). Kept because some Samsung One UI builds still heuristically read it, and
             a deprecated-but-valid attribute costs nothing.

         ⚠ Neither one does anything while the package declares TWO launcher activities: a game
         launcher classifies an app, not an icon, and an app with two MAIN/LAUNCHER entries is ambiguous
         to it. That second activity is gone below, which is half of why this now takes effect. -->
    <application android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"
                 android:appCategory="game"
                 android:isGame="true">
        <!-- ✅ `BL-46` CLOSED 2026-08-14. The dead UnityPlayerActivity block that used to sit here is
             DELETED. History, because the reasoning is not obvious from the absence:

             Our Application Entry is GameActivity (ProjectSettings androidApplicationEntry: 2), so that
             activity was merged in with android:enabled="false" — yet it KEPT its LAUNCHER intent-filter,
             and the phone drew a SECOND app icon. It was kept ON PURPOSE from 2026-08-02 as the duo-
             testing rig, because two icons appeared to give two independent clients.

             That reading was half wrong, and the device has now settled it. After a phone update the
             second icon stopped appearing at all, and the owner runs his two side-by-side clients from
             **Secure Folder** — a Samsung profile clone with its own UID and data dir, which is what was
             really providing the second client the whole time (as the old comment suspected). So this
             block was never the rig; deleting it costs no test capability and leaves exactly ONE
             launcher entry, which is what the game-mode classification above needs.

             (NB: a doubled hyphen may never appear inside an XML comment. Spelling the adb user flag
             out here is exactly what broke the 0.47.0 APK build, and Unity fails it at manifest
             generation — after IL2CPP, ~3 minutes in — so it does not look like a manifest problem.) -->
        <!--Used when Application Entry is set to GameActivity, otherwise remove this activity block-->
        <activity android:name="com.unity3d.player.UnityPlayerGameActivity"
                  android:theme="@style/BaseUnityGameActivityTheme">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data android:name="unityplayer.UnityActivity" android:value="true" />
            <meta-data android:name="android.app.lib_name" android:value="game" />
        </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
